            "<strong>Tuberculosis</strong> is caused by <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em>, a slow-growing, acid-fast bacillus that spreads from a person with pulmonary or laryngeal disease in <strong>droplet nuclei</strong> — particles small enough to stay suspended in the air of a room for hours and to reach the alveoli when inhaled. It does not spread by touch, by cups or by food; it spreads by breathing shared air, which is why the crowded, poorly ventilated room, the prison cell and the long queue in a closed waiting area are where it moves. About a quarter of the world's people carry the bacillus in a <strong>latent</strong> state, walled off in granulomas, well and not infectious; five to ten per cent of them will develop active disease in their lifetime, most within the first two years after infection, and the risk rises sharply with anything that weakens cell-mediated immunity — HIV above all (which raises the annual risk to around ten per cent), but also malnutrition, diabetes, silicosis, smoking, alcohol, steroids and cancer treatment, pregnancy and the first years of life. Ghana's 2013 prevalence survey found far more tuberculosis than had been estimated; the World Health Organization now estimates around 44,000 cases a year and the programme notifies around 19,000, so roughly half the people with tuberculosis in Ghana are never diagnosed, keep coughing and keep transmitting. Finding them is the first task of every nurse.",
