          src: "/diagrams/NG-PNC-1889.svg",
          alt: "Antenatal care — what each contact is actually for; eight contacts is not eight repetitions of the same visit: booking at or before 12 weeks, then 20, 26, 30, 34, 36, 38 and 40 weeks. The booking contact establishes the baseline: dates; haemoglobin, blood group and Rhesus status; HIV, syphilis, hepatitis B — all treatable now; blood pressure before 20 weeks. Later contacts look for what changes: blood pressure and urine for pre-eclampsia; fundal height for growth; lie and presentation from 36 weeks; SP for malaria, iron and folate, tetanus cover, a treated net and the birth plan. Why the first contact must be early: anaemia, syphilis, HIV and hypertension are all cheap to find and treat early.",
          caption: "The booking bloods are the triple-elimination programme in a single tube: HIV, syphilis and hepatitis B, all treatable in pregnancy, all preventable in the baby — TLD for life, benzathine penicillin at least thirty days before delivery, tenofovir from 28 weeks when the viral load is high, and the birth dose of hepatitis B vaccine before the baby leaves the labour ward.",
